* 20 Aug 26 Windows port - $INTERNAL, AND IT IS NOT A PRIVILEGE HANDED TO THE
*   CALLER.  SCRAM's client half needs PBKDF2, HMAC-SHA256, SHA-256, XOR and a
*   nonce, and every one of those is an SDEXT key - which BCOMP admits only to
*   a program compiled $internal (BCOMP's int.intrinsics list).  So the class
*   has to carry the flag or it cannot log in at all.
*
*   WHAT THAT DOES AND DOES NOT GRANT.  HDR_INTERNAL is a property of THIS
*   object code, not of whoever calls it: an ordinary program that does
*   obj->connect(...) gains nothing it did not have.  The class calls no
*   KERNEL function, sets no administrator flag and executes nothing locally -
*   execute() and call() send their argument to the REMOTE server down the
*   socket.  The one thing that would make this dangerous is a method that ran
*   caller-supplied code in this process, and there is none.
*
*   IT ALSO CHANGES HOW THIS FILE IS COMPILED: $internal is admitted only to a
*   session that is both internal mode and administrator (BCOMP, 13 Aug 26),
*   so recompiling SDCLIENT now takes "sd -internal" from an elevated window,
*   as APISRVR and the credential subroutines already did.
$internal
class sdclient
$catalogue !sdclient

$include keys.h
$include err.h
$include int$keys.h

$define CX.DISCONNECTED   0    ;* No session active
$define CX.CONNECTED      1    ;* Session active but not...
$define CX.EXECUTING      2    ;* ...executing command (implies connected)

